+{"id": "mann-k_inbox_201", "subject": "USTeleNet Transaction", "email_body": "Just to follow-up on our discussion yesterday regarding the accounting treatment assuming that USTeleNet owns the switch and that we purchase the switch and lease it back to them under a \"capital\" lease, the following would occur:  The asset would stay on their books (however it would be \"marked-up\" to FMV as evidenced by the purchase price) They would defer the gain, if any, over the asset's life.\nThey would record a payable on their balance sheet and recognize interest expense in their P&L as payments are made We would record the payment of cash to them and book a receivable and deferred interest income (to be recognized over the lease term)  Essentially we are lending them money equal to the FMV of their asset and receiving interest payments in return (i.e.\ndirect financing lease)  I would be happy to go into more detail at your request.\nPlease let me know as issues develop and I will assist as necessary.\nRegards,", "author": "unknown.sender@external.com", "to": "mann-k@enron.com"}
